"""Jack Marston, reference fiche, PART D: base data, Development and performance,
Reception.

His live page (characters/jack-marston/) was generated by scripts/_wave1.py and is not
in _enrich.py: the base below is rebuilt from that entry, with corrections. Errors and
weak points fixed from the old page: "Bookish and gentle where his father was hard" and
"the boy the whole Marston tragedy is meant to protect" (opinion, no source); "a
sensibility his father never quite understood" (interpretation); "takes up the gun he
never wanted" (no source); "doted on by the camp" (vague); the Reception text ("Jack
divides players: some resent leaving Arthur and John behind for him...") had no source
and is replaced by dated, attributed articles; the "Behind the scenes" claim that the
switch "has stayed with fans ever since" (unsourced) is replaced by Dan Houser's own
account; the "Survivor" chip (vague) removed; the full name (John "Jack" Marston Jr.,
from the credits), the actors, Undead Nightmare and Liars and Cheats added. Eyebrow
"Marston family" matches characters_registry.py. publishDate 2026-06-25 kept (git:
2486f0d, drip publish of jack-marston, 25 Jun 2026).

Sources (internal register, fetched 19 Oct 2026, saved in scratchpad cov/jack/fc/):
- Red Dead Redemption manual (Rockstar, 2010; archived PDF, rdr1_manual.txt): cast list
  "John “Jack” Marston Jr. ... Josh Blaylock"; cutscene dialogue directed by Rod
  Edge. Red Dead Redemption GOTY credits (archive.org PDF, OCR in rdr1_credits.txt):
  "John "Jack" Marston" / Josh Blaylock in the cast block.
- Wikipedia: "Josh Blaylock" (born 29 March 1990, Plano, Texas; BrianD in Video Game High
  School, 2012 to 2014; Jack in Red Dead Redemption and Undead Nightmare, IMDb refs);
  "List of Red Dead Redemption 2 characters" (Jack section: Buccianti young child,
  Sutherland pre-teen, citing Polygon; Mashable quote); "Red Dead Redemption" (cast;
  themes paragraph citing Guardian, GamesRadar, A.V. Club, Game Informer).
- Polygon, Cass Marshall, 3 May 2019 (Buccianti and Sutherland, a child for the early
  motion capture; Moore: the boy was about 5).
- Red Dead Wiki: "Jack Marston" (infobox voices, birth year from his age in 1907, Liars
  and Cheats model, trivia), "Josh Blaylock", "Marissa Buccianti", "Ted Sutherland",
  "Red Dead Redemption 2 Cut Content" (outfits, jck1, Kieran stable encounter, Cain at
  Horseshoe Overlook, "Uncle Dutch" encounter, concept art), "Red Dead Redemption Cut
  Content" (sheriff's badges on the US Marshal and Bureau uniforms). Mission page
  "Fatherhood, For Beginners" (riding lesson at Pronghorn Ranch).
- Dan Houser interview with IGN, quotes checked in GamesRadar+, Anthony
  McGlynn, 13 Oct 2026 (yahoo_livid.txt) and Game Rant, Sarah Fields, 13 Oct 2026 (gamerant_houser.txt;
  Viraaj Bhatnagar only signs a related link). The date of the IGN interview itself is not
  given: "reported on 13 October 2026".
- Game Informer: Jill Grodt, 28 Sep 2018 (returning characters); staff, 27 Dec 2018
  (19th of 24); Shin Hieftje, 16 Apr 2014, "What Makes A Game Ending Memorable?".
- GamesRadar+, David Roberts, 2015, "Watch gaming's most satisfying character switches"
  (Wikipedia gives 17 Dec 2015, the archived copy is from Oct 2015: year only).
- The Guardian, Stuart Heritage, 19 Jul 2017. The A.V. Club, 20 May 2020, "Games over:
  The best endings in gaming" (intro quoted, no single author named). Mashable, Jess
  Joho, 7 Dec 2018. Game Rant, Joshua Duckworth, 27 Mar 2020 (Jack and Ross rivalry).
  All quotes checked against the saved pages.
- The Hollywood Reporter, 7 Sep 2018: Rockstar description of Jack (quoted in part B,
  not here).
Excluded: Fred Midgley as the first child actor for Jack's motion capture (Red Dead Wiki
trivia, no source, no credits found); the wiki's claim that Jack's 1899 voice was first
recorded by a male actor (single TikTok reference); Buccianti's hometown and studies,
Sutherland's birth date and screen credits (Red Dead Wiki only; Sutherland has no
Wikipedia page, so the link to the namesake film actor is not verified); Blaylock's
marriages and children (irrelevant); the GamesRadar+ paraphrase "aged up three years to
19" (the outlet's words, not Houser's); Ross's "brother" in the Game Informer summary
(the outlet's wording, not needed); the Game Rant piece's speculation about the Bureau
pursuing Jack after 1914; the RDR2 audio glitch with a Blaylock-like voice (trivia, part
C); the GTA V book by "J. Marston" (part C).
DUPLICATE ownership: Houser's account of the switch to Jack, the actors and all cut
content live HERE; parts B and C must not retell them (C trivia may not repeat). Moore's
memories of the child actor are in abigail_D (other fiche) and only summarized here. The
Rockstar description of 6 Sep 2018 belongs to part B.
"""


def two(en, fr): return {"en": en, "fr": fr}


BLOCKS_DEV = [
    {"h3": two("Casting", "Le casting")},
    {"p": two("In Red Dead Redemption (2010), Jack is played by Josh Blaylock. The cast list printed in the game's manual credits the role as John \"Jack\" Marston Jr.; the end credits of the Game of the Year Edition list him as John \"Jack\" Marston. Blaylock is an American actor, born on 29 March 1990 in Plano, Texas, who later played BrianD in the web series Video Game High School (2012 to 2014). He also voices Jack in Undead Nightmare, the 2010 expansion. The game's motion capture cutscenes and dialogue were directed by Rod Edge. According to the trivia of the \"Remember My Family\" mission page on the Red Dead Wiki, Rob Wiethoff, who plays John, did the motion capture for Jack in his duel with Ross.",
              "Dans Red Dead Redemption (2010), Jack est interprété par Josh Blaylock. La liste des interprètes imprimée dans la notice du jeu le crédite sous le nom de John \"Jack\" Marston Jr. ; le générique de fin de l'édition Game of the Year l'appelle John \"Jack\" Marston. Blaylock est un acteur américain, né le 29 mars 1990 à Plano, au Texas ; il a ensuite joué BrianD dans la websérie Video Game High School (2012 à 2014). Il prête aussi sa voix à Jack dans Undead Nightmare, l'extension sortie en 2010. Les cinématiques en capture de mouvement et les dialogues du jeu sont dirigés par Rod Edge. D'après les anecdotes de la page de la mission \"Remember My Family\" sur le Red Dead Wiki, c'est Rob Wiethoff, l'interprète de John, qui a assuré la capture de mouvement de Jack pour son duel contre Ross.")},
    {"p": two("Red Dead Redemption 2 (2018) recast the role and split it by age. Marissa Buccianti plays Jack as a young child in 1899, and Ted Sutherland plays him at about twelve in the 1907 epilogue. Polygon, in an article by Cass Marshall published on 3 May 2019, explained that the character needed several actors because he grows up over the course of the game, and that a young child also provided the motion capture for the early scenes. Cali Elizabeth Moore, who plays Abigail, told Polygon that the boy who played Jack at his youngest was about five, and that during her first few months on the project only she and the actors playing John and Jack were working together. Rob Wiethoff plays John in both games; Abigail (Sophia Marzocchi, then Cali Elizabeth Moore) was recast, like Jack.",
              "Red Dead Redemption 2 (2018) confie le rôle à de nouveaux interprètes et le partage selon l'âge. Marissa Buccianti joue Jack enfant, en 1899, et Ted Sutherland lui prête ses traits vers douze ans, dans l'épilogue de 1907. Dans un article de Cass Marshall publié le 3 mai 2019, Polygon explique que le personnage a nécessité plusieurs acteurs parce qu'il grandit au fil du jeu, et qu'un jeune enfant a en outre assuré la capture de mouvement des premières scènes. Cali Elizabeth Moore, l'interprète d'Abigail, a raconté à Polygon que le garçon qui jouait Jack tout petit avait environ cinq ans, et que pendant ses premiers mois sur le projet, elle ne travaillait qu'avec les interprètes de John et de Jack. Rob Wiethoff joue John dans les deux jeux ; Abigail (Sophia Marzocchi, puis Cali Elizabeth Moore) a changé d'interprète, comme Jack.")},
    {"h3": two("Making Jack the player character", "Faire de Jack le personnage jouable")},
    {"p": two("Rockstar co-founder Dan Houser, one of the writers of Red Dead Redemption, described the decision in an interview with IGN, reported on 13 October 2026. He discussed the death of John with the game's lead designer, Christian Cantamessa, and both liked the idea. Houser's concern was practical: \"how does an open-world game work if you've killed the protagonist?\" In an open-world game, he explained, players finish the story and then return to the world to complete the side content, so \"We'd have to play as someone else for that.\" The team chose Jack. Houser said the switch raised questions about side characters with arcs of several missions: what would happen if a player had done one mission as John and came back as Jack? \"We had to think through all of that stuff and concluded we could probably make it work, but we were still scared.\"",
              "Dan Houser, cofondateur de Rockstar et l'un des scénaristes de Red Dead Redemption, revient sur cette décision dans un entretien accordé à IGN, relayé le 13 octobre 2026. Il a discuté de la mort de John avec le concepteur principal du jeu, Christian Cantamessa, et l'idée leur plaisait à tous les deux. Son inquiétude était d'ordre pratique : \"how does an open-world game work if you've killed the protagonist?\" (comment fonctionne un monde ouvert si l'on a tué le héros ?). Dans ce type de jeu, explique-t-il, on termine l'histoire puis on revient dans le monde pour achever les quêtes annexes ; il fallait donc pouvoir continuer avec un autre personnage. L'équipe choisit Jack. Selon Houser, ce changement posait problème pour les personnages secondaires dont l'histoire s'étale sur plusieurs missions : que se passe-t-il si le joueur en a fait une avec John et revient avec Jack ? \"We had to think through all of that stuff and concluded we could probably make it work, but we were still scared\" (il a fallu tout repenser ; on a conclu que ça pouvait sans doute marcher, mais on avait quand même peur).")},
    {"p": two("Houser said there was \"broad consensus\" inside the studio that it was the right decision, but that the team was surprised by how angry some players were when the game came out: \"There were people who were livid with you.\" He took that anger as a sign that players had \"really engaged in the story and the characters\". As a writer, he added, he felt John had to die for the story to have weight, something he had wanted in several Grand Theft Auto games but had never done for technical reasons. The interview was reported by GamesRadar+ (Anthony McGlynn) and Game Rant (Sarah Fields).",
              "Houser assure qu'il y avait au sein du studio un \"broad consensus\" (un large consensus) en faveur de ce choix, mais que l'équipe a été surprise par la colère de certains joueurs à la sortie du jeu : \"There were people who were livid with you\" (certains étaient furieux contre nous). Il y voit le signe que les joueurs s'étaient \"really engaged in the story and the characters\" (vraiment investis dans l'histoire et les personnages). En tant qu'auteur, ajoute-t-il, il estimait que John devait mourir pour donner du poids au récit, ce qu'il avait voulu faire dans plusieurs Grand Theft Auto sans jamais y parvenir, pour des raisons techniques. L'entretien a été relayé par GamesRadar+ (Anthony McGlynn) et Game Rant (Sarah Fields).")},
    {"h3": two("Cut content", "Contenu coupé")},
    {"p": two("The files of Red Dead Redemption 2 contain unused clothing for Jack: a pair of pants, a vest, a hat and two coats. A cut epilogue mission labeled \"jck1\" was set at Beecher's Hope and would have opened with John handing Jack a book in his room while John was writing; John would then have taught his son to ride. In the released game, the riding lesson takes place earlier, at Pronghorn Ranch, in \"Fatherhood, For Beginners\". A piece of concept art shows Jack with Arthur, John and Abigail.",
              "Les fichiers de Red Dead Redemption 2 contiennent des vêtements inutilisés pour Jack : un pantalon, un gilet, un chapeau et deux manteaux. Une mission de l'épilogue supprimée, nommée \"jck1\", se déroulait à Beecher's Hope : elle devait s'ouvrir sur John tendant un livre à Jack dans sa chambre, alors qu'il était en train d'écrire, puis John devait apprendre à son fils à monter à cheval. Dans le jeu final, la leçon d'équitation a lieu plus tôt, à Pronghorn Ranch, dans \"Fatherhood, For Beginners\". Une illustration conceptuelle montre Jack aux côtés d'Arthur, de John et d'Abigail.")},
    {"p": two("Three camp encounters with Jack were also cut. At Colter, [[kieran-duffy|Kieran Duffy]], held in the stables, would have tried to bribe Jack into fetching him food; Arthur would have stepped in and threatened to cut Kieran's throat if he spoke to the boy again. Animations suggest that the dog Cain was first meant to join the gang at Horseshoe Overlook, with an encounter in which Jack plays with him there. At Beecher's Hope, Jack would have asked whether Uncle Dutch was coming to visit; John would have answered that he hoped not, told him that Dutch was a bad man, and asked him to stay away from Dutch if he ever saw him again.",
              "Trois scènes de camp avec Jack ont aussi été supprimées. À Colter, [[kieran-duffy|Kieran Duffy]], retenu dans l'écurie, devait tenter d'acheter Jack pour qu'il lui apporte à manger ; Arthur serait intervenu en menaçant de lui trancher la gorge s'il adressait encore la parole à l'enfant. Des animations laissent penser que le chien Cain devait d'abord rejoindre la bande à Horseshoe Overlook, avec une scène où Jack joue avec lui. À Beecher's Hope, Jack devait demander si l'oncle Dutch allait leur rendre visite ; John aurait répondu qu'il espérait bien que non, lui aurait dit que Dutch était un homme mauvais et lui aurait demandé de s'en tenir loin s'il le revoyait un jour.")},
    {"p": two("In Red Dead Redemption, the US Marshal and Bureau uniforms, which only Jack can wear, first carried a sheriff's badge, as their menu icons, a texture and some pre-release screenshots show.",
              "Dans Red Dead Redemption, le US Marshal Uniform et le Bureau Uniform, que seul Jack peut porter, arboraient d'abord une étoile de shérif, comme le montrent leurs icônes de menu, une texture et plusieurs captures d'avant la sortie.")},
]

BLOCKS_RECEPTION = [
    {"h3": two("Before the release of Red Dead Redemption 2", "Avant la sortie de Red Dead Redemption 2")},
    {"p": two("In a Game Informer feature on the seven characters returning from the first game, published on 28 September 2018, Jill Grodt described Jack as \"Curious and bookish\". She wrote that he feels John does not see him as a real man and tries to prove himself with tasks too dangerous for a boy, and that he is good-hearted \"when the angst of youth isn't acting up\". She noted that he calls Bill and Javier his uncles and remembers their adventure stories, a sign to her that he was kept away from the gang's actual crimes. Her profile ends with Jack becoming a frontier gunslinger and taking revenge on Ross, despite his gift for learning and his parents' hopes.",
              "Dans un dossier de Game Informer consacré aux sept personnages du premier jeu qui reviennent dans la préquelle, publié le 28 septembre 2018, Jill Grodt décrit Jack comme \"Curious and bookish\" (curieux et studieux). Selon elle, il a le sentiment que John ne le considère pas comme un homme et cherche à faire ses preuves par des tâches trop dangereuses pour un garçon de son âge ; il a bon cœur, tant que les tourments de l'adolescence ne prennent pas le dessus. Elle relève qu'il appelle Bill et Javier ses oncles et se souvient de leurs récits d'aventure, signe à ses yeux qu'on l'a tenu à l'écart des crimes de la bande. Son portrait se termine sur un Jack devenu pistolero de la frontière et vengeur de son père face à Ross, malgré son goût pour l'étude et les espoirs de ses parents.")},
    {"h3": two("The 1914 epilogue", "L'épilogue de 1914")},
    {"ul": [
        two("Game Informer, 16 April 2014: in a feature by Shin Hieftje on memorable game endings, Red Dead Redemption illustrates \"Closure\". Hieftje describes the search for Ross as Jack and the final duel, and calls the result \"a manifestation of the title: redemption, in a bloody and final fashion.\" He concludes that the game has \"the perfect ending\".",
            "Game Informer, 16 avril 2014 : dans un dossier de Shin Hieftje sur les fins de jeu mémorables, Red Dead Redemption illustre la notion de conclusion (\"Closure\"). Hieftje décrit la traque de Ross dans la peau de Jack et le duel final, qui incarne selon lui le titre du jeu : une rédemption sanglante et définitive. Il juge que le jeu a \"the perfect ending\" (la fin parfaite)."),
        two("GamesRadar+, 2015: in a list by David Roberts of gaming's most satisfying character switches, the move to an \"older, angrier, hairier Jack\" after John's death is called \"brilliant\", because it opens the post-story world and also \"calls directly back to the cyclical nature of violence and tragedy\" that runs through the game.",
            "GamesRadar+, 2015 : dans une liste de David Roberts consacrée aux changements de personnage les plus réussis du jeu vidéo, le passage à un Jack \"older, angrier, hairier\" (plus vieux, plus en colère, plus poilu) après la mort de John est jugé \"brilliant\" (brillant) : il ouvre le monde après l'histoire et renvoie directement à la nature cyclique de la violence et de la tragédie qui traverse le jeu."),
        two("The Guardian, 19 July 2017: Stuart Heritage sums up the ending as a son who finds his father's killer and murders him, and writes: \"The violence is cyclical, and you've failed to break free of it. There never was any redemption.\" He calls the sequence spectacular as storytelling, \"But that's all it is\": the player is funneled down a track that can only end one way, unlike the ride into Mexico, which he calls his favorite moment in any medium.",
            "The Guardian, 19 juillet 2017 : Stuart Heritage résume la fin à un fils qui retrouve l'assassin de son père et le tue, et écrit : \"The violence is cyclical, and you've failed to break free of it. There never was any redemption\" (la violence est cyclique, et l'on n'a pas su s'en libérer ; il n'y a jamais eu de rédemption). Il juge la séquence spectaculaire sur le plan narratif, \"But that's all it is\" (mais rien de plus) : le joueur est conduit sur une voie qui ne peut finir que d'une seule façon, à la différence de l'arrivée au Mexique, son moment préféré, tous supports confondus."),
        two("The A.V. Club, 20 May 2020: a list of gaming's best endings, published for the game's tenth anniversary, devotes its introduction to Red Dead Redemption. The writers consider John's death unremarkable as a story beat, and place \"The revolutionary moment\" in Jack taking up his father's guns to avenge him and becoming the player character. Other media had treated the cycle of vengeance, they write, but \"Red Dead forced its players to live it out.\"",
            "The A.V. Club, 20 mai 2020 : une liste des meilleures fins du jeu vidéo, publiée pour les dix ans du jeu, consacre son introduction à Red Dead Redemption. Les auteurs jugent la mort de John banale en elle-même et situent \"The revolutionary moment\" (le moment révolutionnaire) dans la décision de Jack de reprendre les armes de son père pour le venger, devenant ainsi le personnage jouable. D'autres œuvres ont traité du cycle de la vengeance, écrivent-ils, mais Red Dead Redemption a obligé ses joueurs à le vivre."),
    ]},
    {"h3": two("Jack in Red Dead Redemption 2", "Jack dans Red Dead Redemption 2")},
    {"ul": [
        two("Mashable, 7 December 2018: in a critical review of the prequel, Jess Joho writes that it reveals a flaw the first game hid, namely that Abigail and Jack are \"shallow cardboard cutouts of Wife and Child\". For most of the game, she writes, Jack is \"just a representation of innocence contrasting the cruelty of his surroundings\", before becoming the more fleshed-out character of the first game. She regrets that the prequel never asks how \"a sensitive, shy boy mostly raised on a ranch\" turns into \"the cold-blooded killer of the first game's epilogue\".",
            "Mashable, 7 décembre 2018 : dans une critique sévère de la préquelle, Jess Joho estime qu'elle révèle un défaut que le premier jeu masquait : Abigail et Jack ne seraient que des silhouettes en carton de l'épouse et de l'enfant. La plupart du temps, écrit-elle, Jack n'est qu'une figure de l'innocence opposée à la cruauté de son entourage, avant de devenir le personnage plus étoffé du premier jeu. Elle regrette que la préquelle ne se demande jamais comment un garçon sensible et timide, élevé pour l'essentiel dans un ranch, devient \"the cold-blooded killer of the first game's epilogue\" (le tueur de sang-froid de l'épilogue du premier jeu)."),
        two("Game Informer, 27 December 2018: 19th of the 24 members of the Van der Linde gang in the staff ranking. The staff found Jack interesting from the start, but mostly because of what players know of his role in the first game, and called him \"essentially a callback machine more than a character\" in the prequel.",
            "Game Informer, 27 décembre 2018 : 19e des 24 membres du gang Van der Linde dans le classement de la rédaction. Celle-ci trouve Jack intéressant d'emblée, mais surtout pour ce que les joueurs savent de son rôle dans le premier jeu, et le décrit dans la préquelle comme une machine à clins d'œil plus que comme un véritable personnage."),
        two("Game Rant, 27 March 2020: Joshua Duckworth reads Jack and [[edgar-ross|Edgar Ross]] as a rivalry spanning both games. He points out that, in chronological order, Ross's first words are addressed to Jack, then four, during the fishing trip of \"A Fisher of Men\", and that his last words, in 1914, are addressed to the grown Jack, who confronts him while he is out by the river.",
            "Game Rant, 27 mars 2020 : Joshua Duckworth voit entre Jack et [[edgar-ross|Edgar Ross]] une rivalité qui couvre les deux jeux. Il relève que, dans l'ordre chronologique, les premiers mots de Ross s'adressent à Jack, qui a alors quatre ans, pendant la partie de pêche de \"A Fisher of Men\", et que ses derniers mots, en 1914, s'adressent au Jack adulte, venu l'affronter au bord de la rivière."),
    ]},
]


